{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1526240-does-anyone-want-more-details-on-the-3rd-generation","id":1526240,"title":"Does anyone want more details on the 3rd generation?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-12-02T11:05:50-08:00","id":1526240,"title":"Does anyone want more details on the 3rd generation?","content":"Or do you strongly feel that they should be kept mysterious and their backstories as shadowy as possible with some truths mixed in? Just a fun idea for a debate of some sort.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-12-02T12:35:56-08:00","id":1526245,"title":"","content":"More detail never really hurt<br /> I mean we have quite fleshed out Antediluvians, like Saulot, Absimiliiard, Cappadocius to a degree, the Eldest or Arikel and Ishtar<br /> Not to mention Sutekh (tho' one of the MOST anoying thing regarding him the fact he simply went off the map after 33 AD and no one heard him since - well except that little lore from Children of the Revolution about Nasch\u200b and the Coil of the Lion\u200b and a deep drink from <i>something </i>that had &quot;the body of a man with the head of an unspeakable beast, no sarcophagus in sight, naked but for a simple loincloth&quot;) or Malkalv<br /> But several others are genuine enigma at the best..<br /> One would say, having details about them is like &quot;a God that bleeds&quot; would undermine their misterious quasy mythological status<br /> But I'm in the other camp, having or god forbid letin the characters uncover details about them, understand them and letting them face the terrible truth they were... humans, just like they were once, but time and power made them what are they today is part of the horror<br /> A &quot;humanised&quot; enemy is the most terrible one<br /> Many humans like evil clearly defined. Black and white. To point at someone else and say, without hesitation, \u201cThat person is/was evil.\u201d Shades of gray can make people uncomfortable. And you can never, <i>ever</i> humanize the enemy, because then you might start seeing them as\u2026 human beings. Like yourself. With dreams. Ambitions. Love for their families. And maybe deep down, they\u2019re actually a \u201cnice\u201d person. You know, despite their evil actions, or the worldview they support, or what they do to protect their family.\u200b<br /> Soldiers cannot think, in the heat of battle, of their enemy as a human being, because they could never pull the trigger otherwise, without being a psychopath.<br /> And ultimately, every player is just a soldier in more often than not in soemone else's war - rarely their own<br /> Especially in vampire where clinging to your humanity is important - or on a Path to your vallues<br /> Maybe the Sabbat is trying so hard ot shed their humanity, becasue their noddists uncovered the terrible truth, their main enemies, the Ancients, Elders, Antediluvians... were humans once, and bacme what they are today walking the same path they are on now<br /> Like the footsteps in the desert under the night sky you follow, and realise in a sense you left those footsteps behind...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude0e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-12-02T13:33:22-08:00","id":1526248,"title":"","content":"I'm in the more details camp, but I say that more in the sense of wishful thinking.<br /> Keeping the Antediluvian a big mystery worked better in earlier editions when it was a bigger question if they even really existed, which is ironic given those were also the editions white wolf had printed stats for Level 10 disciplines(the idea a play group would could achieve that or kill one wasn't anathema yet). During late revised(specifically with things like the Gehenna book) it felt more like the writers were trying to have their cake and eat it too by trying to both keep the Ante's mysterious while trying make them into functional characters with motivations and back stories.<br /> It's too late to pull back and make the ante's into unknowable forces again, so might as well just flesh them out as characters.*<br /> <br /> <br /> *I say this with the understanding that should parawolf try to do this, they need to have writing team capable of giving us something on par with a George R. R. Martin series(or at least a Fromsoft lore wiki) to give this kind of character justice. Something they're likely unable to accomplish right now.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-12-02T14:49:01-08:00","id":1526252,"title":"","content":"I think what sorts of details matters a lot.<br /> <br /> I never want to see Ante character sheets. The game also already suffers enough over powerful NPCs having done this and that throughout history that just cuts off PCs from mattering to stories if you want to keep canon in the mix.<br /> <br /> I think you can balance having more idea of who the Antes are/were (after all plenty of NPCs that have met them are active), with mystery. First, don't tell anything from their perspective. Relate everything through specific (not the annoying &quot;nebulous in-character narrator that everyone ignores and treats as the developers/writers saying true things&quot; stuff they did in older books) characters. Plural is important. Have a bunch of different characters with different PoVs and stories where you have to piece together interpretations from second and third hard accounts.<br /> <br /> Building off of that, most of the &quot;definitions&quot; about them should be explicit story hooks. If Arikel is somehow going to impact the story, give us a good selection of things that give players things to do. This lets you make the presence of the Antes feel real, and impactful, without making them the center of things. This also keeps some of the air of mystery. What sort of plots they kick off tells us lots about them, without it being their story.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-12-02T15:05:29-08:00","id":1526256,"title":"","content":"I don't mind details on the ones who have been active in Kindred society within (un)living memory. Cappadocius, Set, Saulot and a few others only vanished or died fairly recently, so there should be some knowledge of what they were doing or memories from still-(un)living vampires who directly interacted with them. But then you have some like Nosferatu that haven't been seen in thousands and thousands of years, and so I don't mind keeping those ones as mystery boxes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-12-02T15:35:27-08:00","id":1526257,"title":"","content":"New details and fleshed out character information is cool, having writers inject modern politics into the games history is less cool.<br /> <br /> Considering the time period when the 3rd generation were embraced, being a farmer is a newfangled job like being a computer programmer was in the 90s. I have my doubts that the writing staff would have much to work with considering that most of the story hooks had not been invented yet and the remaining 7,000 years or more have been torpor related.<br /> <br /> Then again, I am one of the wierdos who still uses Exalted as the WoD prequel setting, which means I already have system and fluff to support the early stages of WoD.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":2},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-12-03T01:36:23-08:00","id":1526269,"title":"","content":"I think details, done well, would be cool. At this point, one big difference between Masquerade and Requiem is that one has a strongly defined metaplot and PNGs and the other doesn't and that's a strong point for both: those who like the metaplot can enjoy Masquerade, and those who want more mystery can run Requiem games - ignoring the other differences between the two games that make different people like this or that or both or neither. When reading a cool story, eventually I would like all the mysteries to come to light. The fact that to this day there is no revealed canon on the Antediluvians and the origins of vampires in detail bothers me. STs will always be free to ignore what's written if they don't like it for their games, but I itch to know what did Lamia actually whisper to Lazarus? What was Set's actual connection with ancient deities, both Egyptian and Greek (since DAV20 brings Typhon, Echidna and Glycon heavily into the Setite mythos and two out of three Setite bloodlines seem to have been more Greek/Macedonian than Egyptian)? What's the deal with the origins of the Laibon and of native American bloodlines? Why do Ravnos and Assamites both have &quot;judge and, if unworthy, kill other vampires&quot; mandates, but in different ways in their clan culture and unique Road of Enlightenment? And so on, the mysteries are many. I want beautiful, surprising, coherent answers that will make sense, click in the mind and make you say &quot;woah!&quot; and then make you want to make those answers somehow relevant to chronicles, even with the already dead antediluvians.<br /> <br /> At the same time, writing such a completion of the metaplot would be a huge task and lead to a disappointing product. It needs great writers, great knowledge of what's been published so far, and great understanding of the community and their feelings, and also of history and likely religion. Is it best perhaps left alone, then?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-12-03T03:25:50-08:00","id":1526272,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1526240-does-anyone-want-more-details-on-the-3rd-generation?p=1526269#post1526269\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">PNGs</div> </div> </div> </div>What does that acronym stand for? I've don't remember coming across it, and searching online is unhelpful when it's a image file format.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-12-03T03:34:08-08:00","id":1526273,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1526240-does-anyone-want-more-details-on-the-3rd-generation?p=1526245#post1526245\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One would say, having details about them is like &quot;a God that bleeds&quot; would undermine their misterious quasy mythological status<br /> But I'm in the other camp, having or god forbid letin the characters uncover details about them, understand them and letting them face the terrible truth they were... humans, just like they were once, but time and power made them what are they today is part of the horror<br /> A &quot;humanised&quot; enemy is the most terrible one<br /> Many humans like evil clearly defined. Black and white. To point at someone else and say, without hesitation, \u201cThat person is/was evil.\u201d Shades of gray can make people uncomfortable. And you can never, <i>ever</i> humanize the enemy, because then you might start seeing them as\u2026 human beings. Like yourself. With dreams. Ambitions. Love for their families. And maybe deep down, they\u2019re actually a \u201cnice\u201d person. You know, despite their evil actions, or the worldview they support, or what they do to protect their family.\u200b<br /> Soldiers cannot think, in the heat of battle, of their enemy as a human being, because they could never pull the trigger otherwise, without being a psychopath.<br /> And ultimately, every player is just a soldier in more often than not in soemone else's war - rarely their own<br /> Especially in vampire where clinging to your humanity is important - or on a Path to your vallues</div> </div> </div> </div>Narratively speaking, I think the feeling of mystery never needs to be forever... A mystery gives you excitement also in anticipation of its being revealed one day or in some way. This allows you to keep trying to uncover it or gives you patience until it's finally revealed. If it is never revealed and it becomes obvious that it never will, it loses all its attractivity and becomes just something you give up on and become emotionally immune to. You might even start feeling that it was bad narrative after all, since the authors managed to create an enticing web of facts and rumours around the Antediluvians, many contradicting each other, but cannot seem to finally display the web in its entirety, justifying how the rumours started and how real, coherent, plausible events led to such incoherent and contradictory legends and myths. This is a bit what I feel: a part of me is excited at the thought of the first generations of vampires because I feel that there is so much potential there if the veil of secrecy is lifted, but another part of me has accepted that a canon, all-explaining narrative will never be published and I therefore give up emotionally on these figures and at best write my own headcanon, but this is not a good thing for my feelings about the game, nor is it good for the publishers, who are losing my interest in this way.<br /> <br /> Game-wise, I have no use for undefeatable foes. I don't even mind if the players become Antediluvian themselves in generation (like Tremere and - maybe - Augustus) or power-scale, as that would mean that their adventures start being on epic Jyhad scale like Cappadocius spending millennia and countless human, vampire, spiritual and material resources in the effort to diablerise God. WoD has no shortage of massively powerful antagonists from other splats and dimensions to make games still interesting even on that power level. After all, it is said that when Cain told [Ventrue] about the Secret Masters, he left the meeting shaken, right?<br /> <br /> As for your point about humanising enemies... I agree with you. It improves and deepens the drama. It makes these enemies more relevant to the PCs, as well as relatable. It gives more reasons why they should have such charisma as to lead their clans. I don't necessarily agree on the philosophical, out-of-game analysis of the psychology behind knowing your enemy is human in the real world, but that's a whole other thing not for this forum.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-12-03T11:46:23-08:00","id":1526283,"title":"","content":"I do like the notion of having the stories of the 3rd generation being told from different point of views such as their eldest childer or great grand childer. I certainly feeling a burst of creativity about the possible details of the Tzismce anti told by the educated guesses of one of his fifth or sixth generation childer.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-12-03T14:48:05-08:00","id":1526297,"title":"","content":"What I would like is not so much the very detailed lore that we've been given about some of the Antedeluvians (particularly in the Revised era) that exists for some metaplot reason, but some general stories, myths, and legends about each of the Antedeluvians that have someone managed to been preserved and passed down, especially in regards to their own clan as well as memories preserved by other (perhaps rival, but not limited to them to clan.<br /> <br /> So what is important to me is not &quot;secret lore&quot; that I as a <i>player</i> would know, but legends that my <i>character</i> (or NPCs) might have heard.<br /> <br /> I think it would be a disservice to the game if there were official information provided that robs the setting of its mysteries. It would make the game setting very sterile. At this point, it's better to leave that to the STs to determine that for their own chronicles. It's better instead to provide IC legends - which may or may not reflect truth or facts - that might inspire STs to create their own version of truth in their own setting. Let a thousand flowers bloom.<br /> <br /> At this point, any outright OOC information is simply fan fiction. I'd be interested in knowing what the original creators of VtM may have intended (Mark Rein-Hagen and Andrew Greenberg), but much less interested in what Justin Achilli would want as canon, and none at all from anybody new. It's like looking for new information about Conan and the Hyborian Age, but not from his creator Robert E. Howard, but instead from one of the pastiche writers of the 1970s or, worse, from some brand new author publishing today.<br /> <br /> For example, knowing some legends about the Ventrue Antedeluvian as known by the Ventrue Clan would be interesting. We have some of these in the two clanbooks, but some explicit tales would be good. Furthermore, it would be good to see some stories told from the perspective of some &quot;traditional ally&quot; clans like the Malkavians and Cappadocians. Then some stories that enemy/rival clans like the Brujah, Lasombra, and Setites. And then some from clans that were never outright allies, but also not really enemies like the Toreador, Gangrel and Nosferatu. These stories shouldn't be contradictory - they should seem to be talking about the same events or same person, but from a different perspective and in a way that seems to relevant to its intended audience (the clan whose part of tradition it is, every clan wouldn't be so much interested in what that story about Ventrue means for the Ventrue clan, but why their clan kept alive this tradition - it must have somehow been important for them.).<br /> <br /> Likewise, I think it would be interesting to see different takes on these tales. One person treats it like some historical text that represents some real history and actual events. Another person treats it more like mythology so it may contain some kind of truth, but shouldn't be taken literally. Another examines it from a Noddist perspective which may be more about some secret, gnostic, or esoteric knowledge - it's a riddle to be deciphered for some greater truth. Then there are stories that are known, but would be dismissed as mere fable or entertainment by yet another person. Or tales interpreted not so much from a clan perspective, but from varying groups who operate at a higher Jyhad level - the True Black Hand, Children of Osiris, and Gehenna Cults like the Servitors of Irad or Ways of the Ancient Lawgivers.<br /> <br /> Then do those for the other Antedeluvians. For example, it would be interesting to see from the perspective of the Brujah Antedeluvian because such stories are probably stories both about Ilyes (the actual Brujah antedeluvian) and about Troile (the pretender who slew her sire). Having a mix of stories about &quot;Brujah&quot; repeated by the Brujah clan would be interesting because some stories would present a very different kind of Antedeluvian, and how this contradictory information is handled by the clan would be interesting (since in lore the idea that Troile is not really the founder isn't there, and the legends of some other founder and the existence of possible True Brujah is just supposed to be rumor). Then we'd get a Ventrue perspective with one tale acting as if Troile was actually the founder, and then another that many Ventrue believe is proof that the True Brujah are a very real thing.<br /> <br /> Some Antedeluvians should have more stories about them than others. Ventrue likely has some of the least since he was supposed to be killed so early. But Antdeluvians who were more active (like Lasombra and Cappadocious) should have more simply because had did more activities including some in the historical era.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}